SECTION I. 
Introduction 
 
The Facility for Antiproton and Ion Research (FAIR) is an international project aimed at building a new heavy ion particle accelerator in Darmstadt, Germany, to produce intense secondary particle beams of unstable nuclei or antiprotons [1] . The particles will reach energies up to 1.5 GeV/u with intensities of 10 12 ions/s. Among the different particle accelerators composing FAIR [2] , there is the Superconducting FRagment Separator (Super-FRS); an in-flight separator which will generate and spatially separate rare isotopes from all elements to be studied in various experiments installed down its three branches (See Fig. 1 ). 
