CEA has completed the design studies of the 21 standard 50-ton Super-FRS dipole magnets [3] and is now focusing on the more complex 3 superferric branched dipole magnets conceptual design. 
 
As the Super-FRS needs to have the ability transport the beam and guide it to the different experimental areas, 3 of the dipoles will have Y-shaped vacuum chamber in their aperture each allowing two possible exit trajectories for the ion beam. These Y-shaped vacuum chamber have a greater volume when compared the standard ones and this will therefore have an impact on the design (see Fig. 2 ) even if most of the existing concept developed so far will be preserved [4] , [5] . 
