B. Field integral homogeneity 
 
The integrated field homogeneity is computed through several steps. First, the reference field integral ( I R e f y = 3.4 T . m ) is integrated over an arc which approximates the beam trajectory with a 12.5 m bending radius arc (ideal field step model) passing through the dipole center (x = 0, y = 0). Then for a given vertical position (i.e., y), the field is calculated over several transverse horizontal positions (i.e., x) so that the integral gradient can be fitted and subtracted from each field integral values. Note that this integral gradient is naturally generated by the trapezoidal shape of the yoke. Finally, these gradient corrected field integral values ( I c o r y ( x , y ) ) are compared with the reference one to get the homogeneity ( ϵ ( x , y ) ) defined by (1) . 
ϵ ( x , y ) = I c o r y ( x , y ) − I R e f y I R e f y (1) 
