C. Conductor and protection 
 
The branched dipole will use the same wire-in-channel NbTi conductor from Luvata [9] as the standard one and its main characteristics can be found in Table III . Even with an operation current increased by almost 20%, the margin on the load line ( ( I c r i t i c a l − I o p e r a t i o n ) / I c r i t i c a l ) remains quite ample as it is reduced down to 74% : it is only a 4% decrease when compared to the standard dipole margin. The conductor has a high Cu/Sc ratio so that the magnet is self-protected during quenches. In case of quench, the energy is dissipated into an external resistor of 4.4 Ohm. When analyzing the worst case fault scenario (lack of detection or failure to open the breakers) where the energy of 3 dipoles connected in series (1.52 MJ, 6 coils) is dissipated into a single quenching coil (no quench in the other 5), the coil reaches a max temperature of 150 K and a max voltage of 1.0 kV (or ± 500 V by connecting a mid point of the dump resistor to the ground). This result was obtained from QTransit, a home-made quench analysis software developed at CEA-Irfu [10] which takes into account the 3D quench propagation velocities [11] . 
