SECTION IV. 
Mechanical Design 
A. Description of the preliminary design 
 
As mentioned previously, the standard cryostat has been split in the mid plane (see Fig. 4 ) leading to two independent cryostats (top with the cryo-tower and bottom). Each cryostat is connected at one of the entrance corners for vacuum, cryogenics, electrical and instrumentation utilities. The continuity of the vacuum between the top and bottom cryostat is realized through a connection made of a bellow located under the cryotower as shown on the left of Fig. 4 . This bellow makes the installation and alignment of the cryostats easier to perform. The cryostats are directly mounted to the yoke through the grey ears (four for each sub-cryostat, see Fig. 4 ). Then one end of each CTWS is mounted on the vacuum vessel while the other is connected to the floating coil casing for alignment and support purpose. Consequently the number of CTWS is doubled for the branched dipole which increases the associated heat load. One advantage of the split cryostat design is that the mid-plane of the coils remains fixed during cool-down: this is not the case for the standard dipole design where the mid-plane of the coils moves up during cool-down. 
