B. Lorentz forces 
 
The branched dipoles have increased Lorentz forces when operated at the maximum operation current. Table IV summarizes the forces on straight and curved coil elements for the standard and branched dipole for comparison purpose. Note that the force components (x, y or z) are defined per the coordinate system of Fig. 5 . The coil element (or conductor) numbers are defined in Fig. 5 . The vertical component of the electromagnetic forces on coils have increased by more than 20% with a strong asymmetry between conductor #1 and #2. These forces also have a non negligible transverse horizontal component also due to lack of symmetry with respect to the longitudinal vertical plane of the magnetic circuit (increased clearance in the yoke for Y-shaped vacuum chamber) and the coil. The observed Lorentz force asymmetries can be attributed to the asymmetric magnetization of the yoke introduced by the additional clearance required to accommodate the Y-shaped vacuum chamber. Fig. 5 shows the magnetic flux density modulus in one half of the yoke to illustrate the asymmetries observed on the Laplace forces (cross product between current density and flux density) for conductor elements #1 and #2 in Table IV . 
