C. Compensation of the forces 
 
Apart from the forces asymmetries, the main difficulty that has to be overcome from the mechanical point of view is the compensation of splitting the standard coil casing on its mid-plane. On the standard version which is similar to existing superferric magnet cryostat designs [12] –​ [14] , there is a common coil casing structure for both coils [5] which restrains them from deforming in the vertical direction. In the case of the branched version, we expect the independent coil casings to be weaker and that the stresses in the stainless steel casings will be too high. In addition to increasing the casings material thickness several possible solutions are currently under study. One of them would be to add a couple of cold-to-warm contacts (CTWC) in the straight element region (#1 and #2 on Fig. 5 ) between the coil groove of the yoke and the coil. These CTWC will probably have to be tunable so that they can be adjusted to be in contact with the coils after the first cool-down operation. The main drawbacks of this solution are that it introduces a tuning step during the magnet commissioning and also that it will draw additional heat load from 300 K to 4.2 K. The impact of these CTWC on the cryogenics is under investigation. 
SECTION V. 
Cryogenic concept 
 
Fig. 6 shows a cross section taken in the longitudinal straight section of the top cryostat (cond #1 on Fig. 5 ). As illustrated by Fig. 6 , the coil is mechanically supported by its coil casing which has a 20 × 10 mm liquid helium channel for keeping its temperature at 4.4 K (1.2 bar). Then, there is a 50 K thermal shield cooled through brazed pipes on its surface in which a 50 K helium gas is circulating. Finally, there will be 30 layers of multilayer insulation (not shown on Fig. 6 ) between the thermal shield and the vacuum vessel. 
