Each coil has an independent helium circuit. Due to the small volume of helium in each circuit (1.3 L) and the need to extract helium vapor (consequence of the heat load) which could accumulate anywhere in each horizontal channel, a helium mass flow has to be imposed. Consequently, this will be done by initiating thermosiphon loops [15] . The principle is the following: the helium density in the channel gets diphasic and lighter than the one in the entrance pipe as it is heated and vaporized by the heat load. If the helium in entrance pipe is heavier (liquid only) than the exit tube (liquid + gas), there is a pressure difference between the entrance and exit of the channel inducing a mass flow. 
 
Unfortunately for design constraint reason, it is not possible to introduce a “goose-neck” at the entrance tubes to get an autonomous (or passive) thermosiphon circulation [15] . Since the entrance and exit pipes are at the same level (horizontal helium channels), an active thermosiphon is implemented by adding a heater on each exit pipe (see Fig. 7 ) to vaporize the helium at the exit pipe and generate the pressure difference necessary to initiate the helium flow described previously. The heat load on the helium channel is about 6 W / m 2 which is barely beyond the lower limit for thermosiphon mass flow stability determined in [16] . 
